<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Appomattox Court House</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Surrender at Appomattox · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Appomattox Court House, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> on 9 April 1865, was the last engagement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-northern-virginia">Army of Northern Virginia</a>. Cut off by Union forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> after the fall of Richmond, Lee surrendered that day on generous terms. The surrender led to the collapse of the Confederacy within weeks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-appomattox-court-house.jpg" alt="Battle of Appomattox Court House" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Nast · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Robert_E._Lee_surrenders_at_Appomattox_Court_House_1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Union Army of the Potomac crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-river">James River</a> in June 1864 and laid siege to Petersburg, south of Richmond. On 1 April 1865 Philip Sheridan's cavalry turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>'s flank at the Battle of Five Forks, and the next day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a>'s army broke through. Lee's men left their trenches on the night of 2-3 April, hoping to join Joseph E. Johnston's Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> in North Carolina. They found no provisions at Amelia Courthouse, and on 6 April nearly a quarter of the army was cut off at Sailor's Creek, where about 7,700 Confederates were captured, among them Lieutenant General Richard S. Ewell. On 8 April George Armstrong Custer captured and burned three supply trains at Appomattox Station.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Grant had written to Lee on 7 April suggesting surrender, and Lee had asked what terms he had in mind. On the morning of 9 April, hoping to break through what he took to be cavalry alone, the Confederate Second Corps under John B. Gordon attacked Sheridan's line and took the ridge, only to find the Union XXIV Corps and V Corps in line of battle. Gordon sent word that he had fought his corps to a frazzle. Lee said there was nothing left but to see General Grant, and he chose the house of Wilmer McLean at Appomattox Court House for the meeting.</p><p>Lee came in his ceremonial uniform; Grant arrived in a mud-spattered one. The terms Grant offered were generous: officers and men were to be paroled, arms and artillery stacked, officers kept their sidearms and horses, and Grant let the men take home their horses and mules for spring planting and provided rations for the hungry army. The document was written out by his adjutant Ely S. Parker, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, and finished around 4 p.m. Grant stopped his soldiers' cheering, saying the Confederates were now countrymen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 12 April about 28,000 Confederates passed by and stacked their arms in a ceremony led on the Union side by Joshua Chamberlain. Chamberlain recalled a salute of arms to the Confederates, and Gordon remembered it too, but the historian William Marvel doubts the account because Chamberlain did not mention it in his letters of the time. Roughly 175,000 Confederates were still in the field. Johnston surrendered to William T. Sherman at Bennett Place on 26 April, Richard Taylor at Citronelle on 4 May, and Edmund Kirby Smith on 2 June; Stand Watie surrendered the last sizeable organised force on 23 June. Jefferson Davis was captured on 10 May.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Appomattox Court House” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
